WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:04.600
Disney have given their animated Aladdin a
live-action makeover.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.399
But is it as shining, shimmering, and splendid
as the original?

00:00:07.399 --> 00:00:12.120
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and today
I'm explaining the top 10 differences between

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.220
the new Aladdin movie and the classic film
from 1992.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:19.180
I'm also kicking off a new giveaway, so subscribe,
hit the bell, and leave a comment about the

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:21.210
movie for a chance to win.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:25.940
Robin Williams' iconic performance as the
Genie in Disney's animated Aladdin is totally

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:29.710
unforgettable and beloved by generations of
children and adults alike.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:34.420
"What better way to make your entrance on
the streets of Agrabah than riding your very

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:36.030
own brand-new camel.

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:37.109
Watch out.

00:00:37.109 --> 00:00:38.109
They spit."

00:00:38.109 --> 00:00:42.969
Obviously, no one could ever hope to recreate
the very specific genius of Williams' madcap,

00:00:42.969 --> 00:00:44.809
impersonation-filled turn.

00:00:44.809 --> 00:00:49.199
So, when it came to retelling that story,
a different approach was needed, and that's

00:00:49.199 --> 00:00:53.769
what Will Smith gives us with his particular
interpretation of the shape-shifting granter

00:00:53.769 --> 00:00:54.769
of wishes.

00:00:54.769 --> 00:00:56.589
"Why you playing hard to wish?"

00:00:56.589 --> 00:01:01.339
Like Williams did so successfully in the animated
movie, Smith brings his own special energy

00:01:01.339 --> 00:01:06.070
to the role of the live-action Genie, injecting
some of his larger-than-life personality into

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:07.070
the part.

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:13.170
"Oh, great one who summons me, I stand by
my oath, loyalty to wishes three.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:15.439
I'm kidding, watch this."

00:01:15.439 --> 00:01:20.130
So expect a genie with some swagger and also
a light Fresh Prince of Bel Air flavour to

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:21.130
his songs.

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:25.079
Smith's charismatic take on the character
soon makes its own impression.

00:01:25.079 --> 00:01:28.030
"And now for your entourage.

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:29.780
Too small.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:31.530
Too obvious.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:35.039
The genie's on fire!"

00:01:35.039 --> 00:01:39.119
He really is a lot fun and gets plenty of
laughs, including some great back-and-forth

00:01:39.119 --> 00:01:41.789
with Aladdin and some of the other palace
residents.

00:01:41.789 --> 00:01:47.080
Mena Massoud gets Aladdin's cheeky charm just
right and has a cool comic rapport with the

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:48.080
Genie.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.080
"Pladam!"

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:50.080
"Do we need the topknot?"

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:51.080
"That's my little cherry on top."

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:54.790
And a lovely on-screen chemistry with Naomi
Scott's Jasmine.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:57.350
"How did you get past the guards?"

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:58.350
"That was challenging."

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.490
"You cannot just break into a palace like
you own the place."

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:04.380
"If you don't have anything, you have to act
like you own everything."

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:08.990
Overall, Massoud shines best in the movie's
comedic moments, so he really makes the most

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:13.030
of some of the additional scenes that Aladdin
gets in this version, including a big dance

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:16.971
number where he's basically orchestrated by
the Genie, and a hilarious scene where he

00:02:16.971 --> 00:02:21.921
meets his future father-in-law and clumsily
tries to charm his way into the Sultan and

00:02:21.921 --> 00:02:22.921
Jasmine's good books.

00:02:22.921 --> 00:02:28.360
As for Aladdin's costume, well, that's undergone
some changes too, with his bare-chested, waistcoat

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:32.050
look in the animation getting an extra shirt
in the new film.

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:36.510
Jasmine's outfit also gets an update to cover
up more in the live action version.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:41.060
Of course, both characters' clothes still
heavily echo their animated attire, and Jasmine,

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:44.140
in particular, gets some stunning new looks.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:48.450
Naomi Scott gives a fantastic performance
as Jasmine, who gets a much more active role

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:51.250
in the new film compared to the animated movie.

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:55.680
In the live-action version, Jasmine's desire
to make her own decisions and choices in life

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:00.040
goes beyond simply wanting to experience a
bit of life outside the palace and marry a

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:01.790
man she actually loves.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:03.390
"How dare you.

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:04.990
All of you.

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:07.420
Standing around deciding my future?

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.620
I am not a prize to be won."

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:13.880
Now Jasmine questions why a woman shouldn't
have political as well as personal power.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:15.600
"You want to be sultan.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.600
But why?"

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:19.450
"I have been preparing for this my whole life."

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:23.630
For example, she asks how a foreign prince
would look after the people of Agrabah better

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:24.630
than she would.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:26.680
"You have no idea what is happening out there.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:27.940
Hakim, tell him."

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:31.200
As she knows and cares for them and wants
to improve their lives.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:35.910
This Jasmine knows she has something to offer
as a leader, not just as a romantic partner.

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:41.120
And we also get some new backstory about Jasmine's
family and why the Sultan is so overprotective

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.480
of his daughter.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:47.250
Alan Menken, Howard Ashman, and Tim Rice's
classic songs from Arabian Nights to Prince

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:51.150
Ali and A Whole New World which you know and
love from the original movie are all there

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:55.510
in the new film, with a few updated lyrics
and a touch of hip-hop on some of Will Smith's

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:56.510
Genie tracks.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:04:01.481
There's also a brand new song for Princess
Jasmine called Speechless, which Naomi Scott

00:04:01.481 --> 00:04:03.330
totally knocks out of the park.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:05.570
"I won't be silenced.

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:08.380
You can't keep me quiet."

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:11.640
Think Frozen's Let It Go meets This Is Me
from The Greatest Showman.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:16.590
In fact, it was written by Disney legend Alan
Menken with Benj Pasek and Justin Paul, the

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:19.670
award-winning duo behind La La Land and The
Greatest Showman.

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:24.480
Oh, and there's a cool curtain call with the
cast busting some moves to Friend Like Me,

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:28.930
and we get a more full-on hip-hop version
of that song sung by Will Smith during the

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:30.630
end credits.

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:34.910
Before the film came out, reactions to the
new Jafar were mixed with some saying he didn't

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:37.480
seem as intimidating as his animated counterpart.

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:38.840
"Bring me the lamp."

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:40.210
"Bring me the lamp.

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:45.290
The rest of the treasure is yours, but the
lamp is mine."

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:49.510
In fact, the movie's production designer revealed
they wanted to make Jafar more evil so during

00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:53.100
reshoots, they "built him a dungeon to do
more nefarious things in."

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:58.160
The new film certainly reveals an even darker
past for Jafar, filling in his backstory before

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:02.470
he became the Sultan's trusted advisor and
providing an interesting comparison between

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:03.880
him and Aladdin.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:08.190
However, this Jafar does still seem to lack
some of the animated villain's menace.

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:14.650
Also, while live-action Jafar uses a snake-headed
staff to mesmerise people like in the animation.

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:15.950
"Don't worry.

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:18.540
Everything will be fine."

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.130
"Everything will be... fine."

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:24.850
He doesn't need disguises to trick Aladdin
into doing what he wants.

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:26.470
"You stumbled upon an opportunity.

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:31.830
There's a cave nearby and in it a simple oil
lamp."

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:36.100
Jafar's parrot side-kick Iago doesn't get
as much screen time in the live-action movie

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:37.980
as he did in the original animation.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:42.200
He does still go out spying on the other characters
for his master in the new film, and even gets

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:47.340
a big action scene, but don't expect to see
him in the sort of crazy slapstick moments

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:49.690
that were a big part of his character in the
1992 movie.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:50.690
"That's him?!

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:56.580
That's the clown we've been waitin' for?"

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:59.780
"You got a problem, Pinky?"

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:03.640
Another difference between live-action and
animated Iago is how they sound.

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:07.800
As voiced by Gilbert Gottfried in the original
animation, he spoke with a regular parrot

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.800
voice when people like the Sultan were around.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:11.460
"I'm at my wit's end."

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:12.460
"Awk!

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:13.460
Wit's end!"

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:17.620
Saving his true wise-cracking, cantankerous
personality for scenes with Jafar.

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:19.710
"I can't take it anymore!

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:25.280
If I gotta choke down on one more of those
moldy, disgusting crackers..."

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:30.200
However, in the live-action version, where
he's voiced by Disney regular Alan Tudyk,

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:33.389
Jafar's feathered friend sticks to typical
parrot talk whenever he speaks.

00:06:33.389 --> 00:06:34.470
"That's what I said.

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:35.560
That's what I said."

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:40.340
Director Guy Ritchie told EW he wanted to
"ground [the film's peripheral characters]

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:42.139
in some degree of reality."

00:06:42.139 --> 00:06:46.640
So I think the movie's squawky parrot voice
is part of adding a bit more of a sense of

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:52.220
realism to the animal characters, in a similar
way to how the mice didn't speak in the live-action

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:53.400
Cinderella.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:57.960
Like Jafar's parrot, Aladdin's pet monkey
is a completely computer-generated creation

00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:59.210
in the new film.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:04.151
Unlike Iago though, Abu gets lots more time
on screen interacting with his owner Aladdin

00:07:04.151 --> 00:07:08.520
and retains a lot of the fun personality of
the original, which is great because he has

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:10.060
some really nice moments.

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:14.400
However, like with Iago, the semi-intelligible
monkey talk from the animation doesn't make

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:17.210
it into the live-action version in the same
way.

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:19.130
"Bleh, bleh, bleh!"

00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:20.130
"What?!"

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:21.130
"Bye, bye!"

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:25.160
The live-action movie introduces several new
characters and my favourite of these is Dalia,

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:29.590
Jasmine's handmaiden and friend who gets some
delightfully funny scenes with not just the

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:31.660
princess, but also Aladdin and Genie.

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:35.390
She's played by actress Nasim Pedrad who's
best known for Saturday Night Live and the

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:39.240
sci-fi comedy People of Earth, which was sadly
cancelled way too soon.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:43.620
Pedrad turns in a scene-stealing performance
as Dalia, really wringing the best out of

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:46.060
every moment she gets on screen.

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:50.169
Jasmine's father, the Sultan, gets a personality
makeover in the live-action movie.

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:54.260
Instead of the bumbling and pompous but generally
good-natured ruler we saw in the original

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:58.740
film
"Have a cracker, pretty Polly.

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:02.660
Your Majesty certainly has a way with dumb
animals."

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:07.750
The new Sultan is wiser and more dignified
"It's a pleasure to welcome you to Agrabah,

00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:08.750
Prince Ali."

00:08:08.750 --> 00:08:13.699
And we also get some new backstory about the
Sultan's wife, Jasmine's mother.

00:08:13.699 --> 00:08:18.040
Instead of the animation's cruel and arrogant
Prince Achmed, who almost whipped two young

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:21.910
children in the street before he was stopped
by Aladdin, the live-action movie gives us

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:24.770
a different potential suitor for Princess
Jasmine.

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:28.910
This new prince, Anders, is from a fictional
place called Skånland and, rather than being

00:08:28.910 --> 00:08:32.390
constantly annoyed like Achmed, he's more
bumbling and idiotic.

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:36.669
However, when Prince Anders meets Jasmine
and Rajah, fans of the original may notice

00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:40.319
a little easter egg to how Jasmine's tiger
reacts to the prince.

00:08:40.319 --> 00:08:44.829
And I'll be delving deeper into those easter
eggs from the original in my next Aladdin

00:08:44.829 --> 00:08:45.829
video.

00:08:45.829 --> 00:08:49.889
I'll add a link on the screen and in the video
description as soon as it's ready.

00:08:49.889 --> 00:08:52.529
Now, it's time for you to decide which Aladdin
is better.

